        "# Lecture 29.2: Local Dependencies\n",
        "\n",
        "## Beyond Independence in Central Limit Theorems\n",
        "\n",
        "The classical Central Limit Theorem (CLT) guarantees that normalized sums of independent and identically distributed (i.i.d.) random variables with finite variance converge in distribution to a standard normal variable. A natural theoretical question arises: *Can we establish a Central Limit Theorem for dependent random variables?*\n",
        "\n",
        "When the dependence structure is local—meaning each variable only interacts with a small neighborhood of other variables—we can retain asymptotic normality.\n",
        "\n",
        ":::{#exm-block-independent-vectors}\n",
        "## Block-Independent Vectors\n",
        "\n",
        "Consider sequence vectors $X_1^n, X_2^n, \\dots, X_d^n \\in \\mathbb{R}$ that are $L^2$ zero-mean random vectors. Suppose we construct a array of variables arranged in \"independent neighborhoods\":\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\\begin{pmatrix}  X_1^1 & X_2^1 & \\dots & X_d^1 \\\\ X_1^2 & X_2^2 & \\dots & X_d^2 \\\\ \\vdots & \\vdots & \\ddots & \\vdots \\\\ X_1^n & X_2^n & \\dots & X_d^n  \\end{pmatrix}$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where across rows $j \\neq k$, the vectors are independent, but within the same row (across columns), variables may be dependent.\n",
        "\n",
        "Define $Y_j = \\sum_{i=1}^d X_i^j$ for each row $j \\in \\{1, \\dots, n\\}$. Since the rows are i.i.d., the row sums $\\{Y_j\\}_{j=1}^n$ are i.i.d. random variables with $E[Y_j] = 0$. Setting $S_{nd} = \\sum_{j=1}^n Y_j$, the standard CLT implies:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\\frac{S_{nd}}{\\sqrt{n d}} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{d}} \\left( \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}} \\sum_{j=1}^n Y_j \\right) \\xrightarrow{d} \\mathcal{N}(0, \\sigma^2), \\quad \\text{where } \\sigma^2 = \\frac{1}{d} E[Y_1^2].$$\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        ":::{#exm-1-dependent-sequences}\n",
        "## 1-Dependent Sequences\n",
        "\n",
        "Let $\\{X_n\\}_{n=0}^\\infty$ be an i.i.d. sequence of random variables. Define a new sequence by $Y_n = X_n X_{n-1}$ for $n \\ge 1$. \n",
        "\n",
        "* $Y_n$ and $Y_m$ are independent whenever $\\vert{}n - m\\vert{} \\ge 2$.\n",
        "* $Y_n$ and $Y_{n-1}$ are dependent because both share the factor $X_{n-1}$.\n",
        "\n",
        "This is a classic example of a **$1$-dependent** sequence.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## Formal Framework: Dependency Graphs\n",
        "\n",
        "To systematically quantify local dependence, we model dependency structures using graph theory.\n",
        "\n",
        ":::{#def-dependency-graph}\n",
        "### Dependency Graph\n",
        "Let $\\{X_j\\}_{j \\in V}$ be a collection of random variables indexed by a finite or countable vertex set $V$. A simple, loop-free graph $G = (V, E)$ is called a **dependency graph** for $\\{X_j\\}_{j \\in V}$ if for any two disjoint subsets $A, B \\subset V$ with no edge in $E$ connecting $A$ and $B$, the collections $\\{X_i\\}_{i \\in A}$ and $\\{X_j\\}_{j \\in B}$ are mutually independent.\n",
        "\n",
        "Specifically, an edge $\\{i, j\\} \\in E$ indicates potential dependence between $X_i$ and $X_j$.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "**Degrees in Dependency Graphs**:\n",
        "\n",
        "* **Vertex Degree:** For $i \\in V$, $\\operatorname{deg}(i) = \\vert{}\\{j \\in V : \\{i, j\\} \\in E\\}\\vert{}$ denotes the number of neighbors of $i$.\n",
        "* **Graph Degree:** $\\operatorname{deg}(G) = \\max_{i \\in V} \\operatorname{deg}(i)$ is the maximum vertex degree of $G$.\n",
        "\n",
        ":::{#exm-graph-degrees}\n",
        "## Examples of Graph Degrees\n",
        "\n",
        "1. **Independent Variables:** If $\\{X_n\\}_{n=1}^N$ are mutually independent, $E = \\emptyset$, so $\\operatorname{deg}(G) = 0$.\n",
        "2. **$1$-Dependent Moving Average ($Y_n = X_n X_{n-1}$):** Each $Y_n$ is connected only to $Y_{n-1}$ and $Y_{n+1}$. Thus, $\\operatorname{deg}(i) \\le 2$, implying $\\operatorname{deg}(G) = 2$.\n",
        "3. **Block Neighborhoods:** A disjoint union of $d$-vertex cliques yields $\\operatorname{deg}(G) \\le d - 1$.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## Variance Bounding Lemma\n",
        "\n",
        "The maximum degree of a dependency graph provides a strict upper bound on the variance of sums of locally dependent variables.\n",
        "\n",
        ":::{#lem-variance-bound}\n",
        "## Variance Bound via Dependency Degree\n",
        "Let $\\{X_j\\}_{j \\in V}$ be zero-mean, $L^2$ random variables with dependency graph $G = (V, E)$. Let $S = \\sum_{j \\in V} X_j$. Then:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\\operatorname{Var}[S] \\le \\sum_{i \\in V} (1 + \\operatorname{deg}(i)) \\operatorname{Var}[X_i] \\le (1 + \\operatorname{deg}(G)) \\sum_{i \\in V} \\operatorname{Var}[X_i].$$\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        ":::{.proof}\n",
        "Since $\\mathbb{E}[X_j] = 0$, expanding the variance yields:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\operatorname{Var}[S] = \\mathbb{E}\\left[ \\left( \\sum_{j \\in V} X_j \\right)^2 \\right] = \\sum_{i \\in V} \\mathbb{E}[X_i^2] + \\sum_{i \\in V} \\sum_{j \\neq i} \\mathbb{E}[X_i X_j].\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "If $\\{i, j\\} \\notin E$, then $X_i$ and $X_j$ are independent, so $\\mathbb{E}[X_i X_j] = \\mathbb{E}[X_i] \\mathbb{E}[X_j] = 0$. Denoting $j \\sim i$ for $\\{i, j\\} \\in E$, the double sum reduces to:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\\operatorname{Var}[S] = \\sum_{i \\in V} \\operatorname{Var}[X_i] + \\sum_{i \\in V} \\sum_{j \\sim i} \\mathbb{E}[X_i X_j].$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Applying the elementary inequality $ab \\le \\frac{1}{2}(a^2 + b^2)$, we get:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\\mathbb{E}[X_i X_j] \\le \\sqrt{\\mathbb{E}[X_i^2] \\mathbb{E}[X_j^2]} \\le \\frac{1}{2} \\left( \\operatorname{Var}[X_i] + \\operatorname{Var}[X_j] \\right).$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Summing over all adjacent pairs:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\\begin{aligned} \\sum_{i \\in V} \\sum_{j \\sim i} \\mathbb{E}[X_i X_j] &\\le \\frac{1}{2} \\sum_{i \\in V} \\sum_{j \\sim i} \\left( \\operatorname{Var}[X_i] + \\operatorname{Var}[X_j] \\right) \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{2} \\sum_{i \\in V} \\operatorname{deg}(i) \\operatorname{Var}[X_i] + \\frac{1}{2} \\sum_{j \\in V} \\operatorname{deg}(j) \\operatorname{Var}[X_j] \\\\ &= \\sum_{i \\in V} \\operatorname{deg}(i) \\operatorname{Var}[X_i]. \\end{aligned}$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Combining terms yields:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\operatorname{Var}[S] \\le \\sum_{i \\in V} (1 + \\operatorname{deg}(i)) \\operatorname{Var}[X_i] \\le (1 + \\operatorname{deg}(G)) \\sum_{i \\in V} \\operatorname{Var}[X_i]. \n",
        "$$\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## Normal Approximation via Stein's Method\n",
        "\n",
        "By combining dependency graphs with **Stein's Method**, we derive explicit Berry–Esseen-type bounds in the Wasserstein metric ($d_{W_1}$).\n",
        "\n",
        ":::{#thm-steins-clt}\n",
        "### Dependency Graph CLT Bound (Ross, §3.2)\n",
        "Let $\\{X_j\\}_{j \\in V}$ be zero-mean $L^4$ random variables with dependency graph $G = (V, E)$. Set $S = \\sum_{j \\in V} X_j$ and $\\sigma^2 = \\operatorname{Var}[S] > 0$. Let $W = S / \\sigma$ and $Z \\sim \\mathcal{N}(0, 1)$. Then the Wasserstein distance satisfies:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\begin{align*}\n",
        "d_{W_1}(W, Z) &\\le \\frac{6}{\\sqrt{\\pi} \\sigma^2} \\sqrt{(1 + \\operatorname{deg}(G))^3 \\sum_{j \\in V} \\mathbb{E}[X_j^4]} \\\\\n",
        "&+ \\frac{(1 + \\operatorname{deg}(G))^2}{\\sigma^3} \\sum_{j \\in V} \\mathbb{E}[\\vert{}X_j\\vert{}^3].\n",
        "\\end{align*}\n",
        "$$\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        ":::{#exm-moving-average-process}\n",
        "## Moving Average Process Bounds\n",
        "\n",
        "Let $\\{X_j\\}_{j=0}^\\infty$ be i.i.d. zero-mean $L^4$ random variables with $\\mathbb{E}[X_j^2] = 1$. Define $Y_j = X_j X_{j-1}$ for $j \\ge 1$.\n",
        "\n",
        "1. **Moments:** $\\mathbb{E}[Y_j] = 0$, $\\mathbb{E}[Y_j^2] = \\mathbb{E}[X_j^2] \\mathbb{E}[X_{j-1}^2] = 1$, and $\\mathbb{E}[Y_j^4] = \\mathbb{E}[X_1^4]^2$.\n",
        "2. **Covariance:** For $i \\neq j$, $\\mathbb{E}[Y_i Y_j] = 0$, giving $\\sigma^2 = \\operatorname{Var}\\left[\\sum_{j=1}^n Y_j\\right] = n$.\n",
        "3. **Graph Degree:** $\\operatorname{deg}(G) = 2$, so $(1 + \\operatorname{deg}(G)) = 3$.\n",
        "\n",
        "Applying @thm-steins-clt for $W = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}} \\sum_{j=1}^n Y_j$:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\begin{align*}\n",
        "d_{W_1}(W, Z) &\\le \\frac{6}{\\sqrt{\\pi} n} \\sqrt{3^3 \\cdot n \\mathbb{E}[X_1^4]^2} + \\frac{3^2}{n^{3/2}} \\cdot n \\mathbb{E}[\\vert{}X_1\\vert{}^3]^2\\\\\n",
        " &= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}} \\left( \\frac{18 \\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{\\pi}} \\mathbb{E}[X_1^4] + 9 \\mathbb{E}[\\vert{}X_1\\vert{}^3]^2 \\right) \\\\\n",
        " &= \\mathcal{O}\\left(n^{-1/2}\\right).\n",
        "\\end{align*}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        ":::{#exm-erdos-renyi-triangles}\n",
        "## Triangles in Erdős–Rényi Random Graphs\n",
        "\n",
        "Let $G(n, p)$ be an Erdős–Rényi random graph on $V = \\{1, 2, \\dots, n\\}$, where each edge $\\{i, j\\}$ ($1 \\le i < j \\le n$) exists independently with probability $p \\in [0, 1]$. Let $X_{ij} \\sim \\operatorname{Bern}(p)$ be the indicator of edge $\\{i, j\\}$.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Basic Graph Statistics**:\n",
        "\n",
        "* **Total Edges:** $H = \\sum_{i < j} X_{ij} \\sim \\operatorname{Binom}\\left(\\binom{n}{2}, p\\right)$ with $\\mathbb{E}[H] = p \\binom{n}{2}$.\n",
        "* **Vertex Degree:** $\\operatorname{deg}(i) = \\sum_{j \\neq i} X_{ij} \\sim \\operatorname{Binom}(n-1, p)$ with $\\mathbb{E}[\\operatorname{deg}(i)] = p(n-1)$."
      ],
